om one that we've created as  developers through the custom elements  API our connected callback is also  really simple we don't need to do very  much at all we just need to add some  click functionality to make button work  and that's pretty much all there is to  it our HTML however looks a little bit  ugly we use the button elements as we  would in standard HTML but we add the is  attribute to it so we say button is  custom button so our HTML has already  grown another attribute which is not too  terrible but it's not as clean in code  terms as we might like it to be Dom  looks pretty much exactly the same  nothing much changes in that translation  and accessibility tree also looks pretty  much the same it still says it's the  thing with a roll of button the text is  still its accessible name and it's still  focusable so effectively what we've done  is just recreate the button element as a  custom element and we can see this work  pretty much like you'd expect a button  to work only this time we'll hear a  screen reader announce the button to  play button and the screen reader picks  up that information from the  accessibility tree it recognizes the  accessible name play and it recognizes  it that it's a button and tells the user  that information there's a problem  though apart from the fact that the HTML  looks a little bit less clean than we  want it to be customized custom elements  are not particularly well supported  they're not supported in WebKit Apple is  pretty firmly opposed to introducing  support for customized custom elements  so we might have to turn our attention  to autonomous custom elements now an  autonomous custom element doesn't extend  an existing HTML element so we can't  inherit all the good behavior from say  button that we just did with the  customized element instead we have to  start pretty much from scratch and that  means we've got to do all the work that  the browser does for us normally for  ourselves  so in constructor  need to set things up a little bit  differently you'll notice that the the  definition is slightly different this  time I'm going to create a toggle button  as well you may have noticed from the  video we just played that that button  looked different when it was pressed but  the screen reader didn't pick up on that  information so we're gonna set about  creating that toggle button so the  definition has changed to be a toggle -  button this time and if we look at the  connected callback there's an awful lot  more going on inside it we need to set  the role just using setattribute so  that's the bit that fills in the  accessibility role we need to set  tabindex to zero that's the bit that  will make our custom element focusable  with the keyboard and we add in an aria  attribute aria-pressed and we set it  initially to false this is the attribute  that will tell screen readers and inform  the accessibility tree that this is a  different kind of button it's one that  can be turned on or off and we'll see  how that develops we have a function  into the click and keyboard event  handlers and we're just gonna switch the  status of the aria-pressed attribute  over so when someone uses the keyboard  or the mouse to interact with the button  will flip the value to true while it's  pressed and flip it back again to false  when it's not pressed and this will  change the accessibility state  information in the accessibility tree so  our HTML looks a lot more simple we just  open and close our toggle - button  elements and we still put the text on  the inside our Dom however it starts to  look a little bit messy we see all the  attributes we've just set inside the  custom elements that have indexed the  role the aria-pressed they actually leak  out from the custom element into the Dom  so unfortunately here things start to  get a bit more messy looking our  accessibility tree has also changed a  little bit the type of the button the  role of the button is now toggle button  in some browsers and some accessibility  API is it's known as a push button so  this is just a different type of button  everything else is pretty much the same  but the state now also has a recognition  of the fact that this button can be  pressed or not pressed so there is more  named States and roll  information available in the  accessibility tree in the browser when a  screen reader or another assistive  technology queries that information from  the browser the experience for the user  changes quite dramatically play toggle  button not pressed pressed not pressed  so we now have a custom element that is  a toggle button it has all the  accessibility characteristics that we  need to inform something like a screen  reader that it's a toggle button and the  user now gets to understand when that  button is pressed and when it's not so  the experience is a lot better for  someone who can't see the visual state  of the button changing here's where we  come to that experimental JavaScript API  I mentioned the AOM is still relatively  new it's being developed in the web  platform incubator community group ycg  at the w3c and it's being worked on by  people from Apple Mozilla and Google and  it's a JavaScript API that will let us  get involved in that relationship that I  mentioned in the very first slide that  until now has only happened within the  browser so up till now the accessibility  tree has only ever been created by the  browser and the only thing that has  access to that accessibility tree other  than the browser our assistive  technologies like screen readers for the  first time now as developers through the  AOM JavaScript API we're about to get  access to that tree ourselves and that  opens up a lot of different  possibilities and also a few challenges  and things that we're going to need to  be aware of phase one is going to  introduce Aria reflection it means we're  going to be able to set different roles  and attributes and have them reflected  back so it keeps things much simpler and  lets us use more familiar JavaScript  syntax so for reflecting the attributes  there are two interface mix-ins that  have actually been added not only to the  AOM specification but they will also be  added to one point two of the Aria  specification Aria is accessible rich  Internet applications and these two  interface mix-ins will let us reflect  back rolls and other Aria attributes  so if we rewrite our connected callback  for the toggle button we can see the  change in syntax instead of having to  use set attribute we can use element dot  roll equals button to set the the role  of the element we can use element dots  tabindex equals 0 and element  aria-pressed 'false notice though that  the way tabindex &amp; aria-pressed written  has changed there's now a capital I on  the index of tabindex where there  wouldn't normally be and the dash has  gone from the Aria dashed pressed  attribute and instead it two uses  camelcase  so all of these Aria attributes have a  slightly different way of being written  but they achieve pretty much the same  thing we set all those properties and  characteristics exactly as we did in the  earlier example we can also or we will  be able to set element references now  this is where we get into the part that  is still so experimental it doesn't  actually exist in the browser's yet not  even behind the flag but what we'd be  able to do is where we have an element  sorry an attribute like Aria described  by which up till now you can use to  point to another piece of content on the  page using its ID value as the reference  point and it will associate that piece  of text with the first element so  typical example is you focus on a form  filled with a screen reader you'll here  the label of the form field announced  the type of the form field announced and  then you could use Aria described by to  associate an extra hint to help someone  fill out that information and the screen  reader would automatically read that out  to putting ID references on things is  often quite heavy weights you can have  hundreds sometimes thousands in a heavy  application of ID references on a page  but what will happen in the future is  that we'll be able to use just plain  JavaScript element references in our  code instead of having to use ID  references so for example we'll be able  to use Aria described by elements to  feed it one or more reference points for  the information we want to hook up in  the user interface but as I say this is  experimental yet we can't play around  with this feature if you're curious  there is a series of harness of web  platform tests available for  one a at the moment if you take a look  at it in Chrome or the new edge chromium  beta you'll find that about 65 out of  the 95 tests are currently passing so  that's all the attribute and role  reflections but none of the element  reflections Safari is doing really well  with about 79 interestingly WebKit  Safari has taken out from behind the  flag so this works actually at the  production level in Chrome it's still  behind the AOM accessibility flag  Firefox hasn't yet introduced any  support behind the flag or otherwise but  I'm pretty sure it will catch up soon so  hitting those tests in your browser is a  pretty good way to keep up to date with  how much of phase one is currently  available to play with phase one B  introduces custom element semantics now  the idea behind this phase is that we  can start to clean up some of that Dom  leakage that we saw with our toggle  button example we don't want all the  tabindex the role the Aria press to the  other attributes to leak out into the  Dom when we create a custom element we  want to keep the accessibility  encapsulated so this is the part of the  spec that will let us do that in two  different ways ultimately we'll be able  to set accessibility characteristics at  the time the custom element is created  and ones that can be adapted and changed  in the element internals so for example  we could change our code slightly so  that element definitions options include  things like setting the role and the  tabindex because we want those to be  established in the custom element the  moment it's created exactly as though we  were using a standard HTML button  element we can then do per instance  semantics using an internals variable we  can attach the internals in the  constructor for the custom element and  that lets us set things that will change  through the course of the custom  elements lifetime so aria-pressed will  change in response to user interactions  so we set that here instead of as an  immutable characteristic in the element  definition options but as I say  unfortunately at the moment we still  can't play with this just yet  phase two is all about user interactions  and events we're all really used to  events like click key down key press a  whole bunch of others but when you  introduce an assistive technology like a  screen reader into the equation things  get a little bit more complicated so  they are going to change the way that  events are synthesized and also  introduced some new events the reason  for the synthesis is to protect the  privacy of assistive technology users so  if they were just to create some  assistive technology events it would  very very easy to detect not only that  the user was using a particular  assistive technology but what type of  assistive technology they were using and  that of course would make it very easy  to determine that the person was blind  because they're using a screen reader  had mobility impairments because they're  using speech recognition and so  obviously it's important that we protect  that user privacy so what happens is  when someone using a screen reader  carries out an action it will synthesize  a click event for example so  particularly on touch devices if you tap  a device with a screen reader running  it's a double tap gesture not a single  tap that you'll be used to but that will  synthesize a click event and similarly  other events are cloaked if you like  using these synthesized events they're  also going to introduce some new events  particularly increment and decrement  scroll up scroll down and dismiss and  this very much plays into touchscreen  devices at the moment it's pretty much  impossible to create a custom slider  using all the accessibility  characteristics that are available and  to have it usable with a screen reader  on a touch device the reason is that  when you have a screen reader running on  your touch screen flick up and flick  down the gestures you'd probably use to  adjust the value of a slider are already  used for screen reader specific commands  so there's no way that those commands  can be separated out into using a slider  they're always protected they're always  ring-fenced for the screen reader so if  you go to something like the Aria  authoring practice  guide and pick up the blueprint design  pattern for a slider there implement it  perfectly but try to use it on a  touchscreen device with the screen  reader running it won't work the slider  can't be adjusted in the way that you'd  expect so these are being put into  practice these new events will come  along to help us get around that problem  phase 3 will let us introduce virtual  accessibility nodes into the tree this  is where we really start to get our  hands on that browser only functionality  will be able to add entirely new nodes  into the accessibility tree up until  this point there is always a  corresponding Dom node for an  accessibility node but we can change  that this is the bit where it starts to  feel really scary what we don't want to  happen is for developers to start  creating entirely secondary  accessibility trees just for screen  reader users that bear no resemblance to  the Dom good practice is always going to  be to try and keep those two things  synchronized with each other but one  really clear use case is doing something  with the canvas element when we draw  something to the screen using canvas  it's all done in JavaScript so there are  no HTML elements there's no  accessibility semantics that are  available to the accessibility tree and  so two screen readers so one use of  being able to add virtual nodes to the  accessibility tree is that we can attach  a shadow root to the canvas element we  can then start adding a virtual branch  of the accessibility tree in there to  provide fullback content specifically  for people who can't see the JavaScript  rendered content inside the canvas  element but this is a long way off now  and there's a lot of obstacles to  getting to this point but it really does  have some interesting possibilities  phase four lastly is probably the most  complicated of all the phases it's going  to give us access to all computed  accessibility properties in the  accessibility tree both for the  accessibility nodes that are associated  with Dom nodes but also for the entire  accessibility tree we'll be able to walk  through the accessibility tree in the  same way that we can walk the Dom now so  again it's going to open up a lot of  possibilities for functionality and  capability but I think they're keeping  this one till the last phase because it  is the one that's going to take the most  discussion the most thinking  so looking at those things this is how  we can think about building  accessibility into our custom elements  recognizing that if we use autonomous  custom elements we need to add in the  accessibility characteristics for  ourselves and that the AO M in time is  going to give us a lot more flexibility  to do that but we can also use custom  elements in fact the whole web component  stack to solve accessibility problems as  well sorry so we can create a speak  content element for example I use a  screen reader and one thing I quite  often do is if I want to look at the  news I will go and set my screen reader  reading a news item on the website and  then I'll go off and make myself a cup  of tea while I'm still listening to it  because I don't need to look at the  screen my screen reader on my headset is  portable I can just walk around the  house put the kettle on make the tea and  still listen to the news story and I've  often thought this would be a really  useful thing for everybody to be able to  do being tied to your chair to your  computer your laptop is not always the  ideal experience so perhaps we can use  custom elements to create a widget that  will let us have a button on screen that  will read a designated piece of content  out loud and it turns out it's  reasonably easy to achieve we can use  the HTML template element to set up the  actual element we'll chuck some styling  in there just to make the the button and  the div look good we will also use some  slots to manage things so in the  template we've got a button element that  contains a slot element and this will  let us pull through content from the  light Dom from the the developer code  and we'll also set up a div that's also  going to be a slot for the content that  we want to be spoken out loud  in our connected callback we will do all  sorts of things we will grab the control  the thing we'll hit to make it speak  we'll grab the element that contains the  content and then from there we'll grab  the content that we want to be spoken  out loud in our function we will set up  the web speech API to make it talk we'll  create  since this is object and will attach to  that to the text that we want spoken out  loud that we've grabbed from the light  Dom and then lastly we need to set a  timeout because what we want to do is  lastly make the button look like it's  been pressed for as long as the contents  being spoken and then we want to return  it to its default States when it's  finished we're using the aria-pressed  attribute again to make sure that that  happens in the accessibility state as  well as the visual state reset state  then just checks after 500 milliseconds  and it checks to see if the speech uh  pterence is still talking if it is still  talking it resets the timer for another  500 if it isn't then it returns the  state of the button back to non pressed  would you one last bit of under the hood  magic and we're going to attach the  visual state of the button to the  attribute selector using aria-pressed so  as the value of aria-pressed is changed  the visual style of the button changes  accordingly and it just makes the whole  thing sync up a lot more easily under  the hood and what we get at the end of  it is a very simple piece of text on  page a button that we can hit and  hopefully everything all works for  everybody one tequila two tequila three  tequila floor and so custom elements  hopefully this has given you a brief  insight into can be used not only as I  said to solve accessibility problems but  if we get them right add in all the  accessibility properties that we need to  they give us a really powerful way to  create new and useful features on the  web I need to really thank a couple of  people Scott O'Hara and Patrick Walker  and Dan Hopkins who all gave me a  tremendous amount of help while I was  putting this together there's a URL  on-screen from Pat which leads to a way  to implement a slider that will work in  accessibility terms on touchscreen  devices you basically use a native  slider element you hook it up to the  custom visuals that you want to make  sure you keep it all in sync but if  you're curious about an interim solution  until the aom arrives  that's a really good one to check but  otherwise I recommend that you follow  all of these people they're all damn  good developers in their own right and  pretty damn good people too and lastly  thank you very much to all of you  [Applause]

